instr_linux() {
cat <<'TPL_I_LINUX'
WireGuard client bundle for @NAME@ (config: @CFG@) - Linux

Files: @CONFFILE@ (tunnel config), install.sh, wg-undo.sh, uninstall.sh.

1. Copy all four files into one directory on the target machine.
2. Install: sudo ./install.sh   (sh install.sh also works - it re-execs bash)
   - installs wireguard-tools via apt/dnf/pacman (skipped when present)
   - installs the config as /etc/wireguard/@IFACE@.conf (0600), the undo
     helper as /etc/wireguard/wg-undo.sh, and the DNS companion file
     /etc/wireguard/@IFACE@.dns (containing @DNS@)
   - the conf deliberately carries NO "DNS =" line: wg-quick's resolvconf
     path is missing or dead on minimal systems and its failure kills the
     tunnel. wg-undo.sh applies DNS at PostUp instead - resolvectl when the
     daemon is reachable, else a recorded in-place /etc/resolv.conf write,
     else skipped with a NOTE - and the tunnel comes up in every case; the
     down replay restores DNS exactly
   - validates the conf statically (wg-quick strip) and probes interface-
     creation capability with a throwaway name; it never activates the
     tunnel, so it is safe in image builds and provisioning pipelines
     without NET_ADMIN
3. Activate (separate, deliberate step, on the running system):
   with systemd:    systemctl enable --now wg-quick@@IFACE@
   without systemd: wg-quick up @IFACE@
4. Verify: wg show @IFACE@ (expect a handshake within seconds), then
   ping @SERVER_TUN_IP@.
5. Remove: sudo ./uninstall.sh - downs the tunnel (PreDown replays the undo
   file), removes the config; wireguard-tools stays installed.

Destinations routed into the tunnel: @ALLOWED@
Endpoint: @ENDPOINT@ (the public port is already correct - do not change it)

Extra routes that wg-quick does not cover (for example a subnet reached via a
second gateway INSIDE the tunnel) belong in /etc/wireguard/@IFACE@.extra-routes,
one per line: 'CIDR' or 'CIDR via GATEWAY'. PostUp applies them through
wg-undo.sh so each one is recorded first and removed again on the way down.
Never add such routes by hand - unrecorded routes are exactly what outlives a
tunnel and breaks the network afterwards.
TPL_I_LINUX
}

undo_linux() {
cat <<'TPL_U_LINUX'
Undo data: /var/lib/wireguard-undo/@IFACE@.undo.jsonl - one JSON line
{action, ..., prev} appended BEFORE each change (extra routes, sysctls, DNS).
PreDown runs 'wg-undo.sh down @IFACE@', which replays the file newest-first:
route-add entries are deleted (already-gone is fine, not an error), sysctl
entries are restored to their recorded 'prev', resolv-conf entries restore
the EXACT prior bytes of /etc/resolv.conf from the recorded prev_b64 (in
place, never a rename - container resolv.conf is often a bind mount), and
dns-resolvectl entries revert per-interface DNS where possible (that state
dies with the interface anyway) - then the file is removed. Running down
twice is clean, and a route that is not in the file is never touched.
Routes that wg-quick installs itself are deliberately NOT recorded:
wg-quick removes its own work on the way down.
TPL_U_LINUX
}

